<http://model.geneontology.org/6ab067da00001730> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                                                  owl:versionIRI <http://model.geneontology.org/6ab067da00001730> ;
                                                  <http://geneontology.org/lego/conforms_to_gpad> "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
                                                  <http://geneontology.org/lego/modelstate> "development" ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/contributor> "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7646-0052"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/date> "2026-09-30"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/title> "Endocytosis in IAV entry via TRIM62-WASH (Human-IAV)"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/pav/providedBy> "http://geneontology.org"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  rdfs:comment "Curated with GO AI HUB. TRIM62 binds the FAM21/WASHC2C head domain and limits WASH-retromer coupling, maintaining a retromer-free WASH pool that supports lipid-raft-mediated endocytosis of influenza A virus; VPS35 and FAM21 act in the opposite direction. From PMID:42763336."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "FAM21 identity: WASHC2C (UniProtKB:Q9Y4E1), not the WASHC2A paralogue; the paper's antibody is Invitrogen PA5-35883 (anti-FAM21C)."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Mechanism modelled: VPS35 (retromer) and FAM21/WASHC2C couple the WASH complex to the endosomal membrane, restraining the pool of WASH available for lipid-raft-mediated viral uptake. TRIM62 binds FAM21 and limits that coupling, maintaining a retromer-free, endocytically competent WASH pool. TRIM62 and VPS35 act antagonistically on WASH1 localisation without changing WASH subunit levels (Appendix S1C, Fig 5F, Appendix S2D)."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Not modelled from this paper: TRIM62's separate post-entry role in NP expression (Fig EV2F); the opposing WASH1/VPS35 roles in cholera toxin B uptake (Fig EV3G,H); GLUT1 Golgi mis-sorting on TRIM62 knockdown (Appendix S2E); the pro-entry contributions of CCDC53, SWIP and Strumpellin (Fig 4, knockdown evidence only); and SFV/vaccinia/VSV infection results (Fig EV2G-I). Clathrin-mediated endocytosis is excluded: EGF uptake was unaffected and transferrin uptake increased on TRIM62 knockdown (Fig 3A,B); macropinocytosis is excluded (dextran uptake unaffected, Fig 3C)."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Source: PMID:42763336 (Gupta K et al., EMBO Rep 2026). Human; A549 lung epithelial cells, with HeLa and HEK293T for interaction assays. Virus: influenza A virus X-31 (H3N2)."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "TRIM62 acts here WITHOUT its E3 ubiquitin ligase activity: the RING point mutant and the RING deletion both fully rescued IAV M1 uncoating in TRIM62 KO cells (Fig EV5D,E), while the PRY-SPRY deletion did not (Fig 6H). No ubiquitin-ligase MF is therefore asserted in this model; the activity captured is non-catalytic protein sequestering via the PRY-SPRY domain binding the FAM21 head domain (Y2H Fig 6B; co-IP mapping Fig 6C,D; MST KD 0.40 uM for full-length TRIM62 and 44.9 uM for the isolated PRY-SPRY domain, Fig 6E,F)."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "WASHC1 is given the root term molecular_function because the paper excludes its canonical activity without identifying a replacement: Arp3/ACTR3 knockdown and CK-666 had no effect on IAV uptake (Fig 3E); TRIM27 knockdown lowered endosomal actin but not IAV endocytosis (Appendix S2G,H); and in TRIM62+VPS35 co-depleted cells viral entry was restored while endosomal actin stayed low (Fig EV4I). The authors raise a non-canonical scaffolding role as a possibility only, and state it remains an open question."^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <https://w3id.org/biolink/vocab/in_taxon> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_9606> .

